Chapter: EffectWidenScopeOut
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

Section: Body

 The unsound direction of scope-to-scope subsumption: a WIDER argument
 handed to a NARROWER parameter.

 `wide-read` may read anything under "/config/". `apply-deep` believes
 what it holds reads only under "/config/app/", and passes that belief to
 its own caller, who granted only "/config/app/". If this compiled, a
 scope would be a suggestion: the argument could read
 "/config/secrets.txt" while every signature in sight said it could not.

 `scope-subsumes` is asked with the PARAMETER's scope as the outer, so
 this is `text-starts-with "/config/" "/config/app/"`, which is False. The
 direction of that call is the whole safety property; reversing it here
 would launder every scope in the tree.

  wide-read : Text -> [FileSystem.Read "/config/", Device.Block] Boolean
  wide-read (p) = file-exists "/config/secrets.txt"

  apply-deep : (Text -> [FileSystem.Read "/config/app/", Device.Block] Boolean), Text -> [FileSystem.Read "/config/app/", Device.Block] Boolean
  apply-deep (f) (p) = f p

  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Read "/config/app/", Device.Block] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (if apply-deep wide-read "/config/app/x" then "y" else "n")
  end
